 "descriptions": [
  {
   "descriptionType": "Abstract",
   "description": "Material samples are the most basic form of data about the natural world. A vast number of scientific material samples have already and continue to be collected. Their collection and study are fundamental for solving many of the societal and environmental challenges facing us today. However, most samples remain invisible, unknown, often uncurated and inaccessible, to researchers-at-large. Remaining in a drawer, a box, in a freezer, or under someone's porch after initial collection is one consequence, or afterlife. Samples are incredibly valuable and they can have more interesting afterlives if they are part of the Internet of Samples (iSamples) and leverage the global research infrastructure. For instance, water samples collected by state and local agencies for regulatory purposes may provide valuable research insights into species in the area through new eDNA analysis or may show the presence of COVID-19 for a local population. There are many other examples of a sample that did not seem so important upon first collection, but if found by the right researcher at the right time, comes to hold a central place in an analysis previously not considered. There are also very interesting afterlives of samples that were collected with and without consent. This talk will highlight exciting work done by members of the Sampling Nature Research Coordination Network, the FAIR Island Project and broader material samples curation community to realize the valuable and interesting afterlives o ..."
  }
 ],
